   "top": {
    "While walking along the Seoul City Wall toward Inwansang Mountain, one will notice a two-story red brick building built in Western architectural style amid the houses in the cozy neighborhood. This house called Dilkusha is a two-story house with a basement floor, and although it has worn down over time, it leaves a strong impression with its dignified appearance and long history. In fact, this particular house was built more than 90 years ago. \r\n\r\nDilkusha was where the news of the March First Independence Movement that traveled across the world originated. And here is the story. \r\nIn 1923, the house was built by an American reporter named Albert Taylor. With a keen interest in the independence movement in Korea, he served as a correspondent for United Press International in 1919 and became the first to inform the world of the March First Independence Movement. \r\n\r\nFor this reason, he was imprisoned at the Seodaemun Prison (present-day Dongnip Park) for 6 months by Japanese authorities. After his release, he built Dilkusha, where he stayed until he was forcibly vacated and deported by the Japanese in 1942. Taylor, who stepped on Korean soil together with his father, considered Joseon his second home. He traveled all over Gyeongseong (present-day Seoul) to take photographs. One day, while walking along the Seoul City Wall, he spotted and became enamored by a massive ginkgo tree, and this was where he later built his house. \r\n \r\nHow Taylor came to deliver the news of the independence movement is quite intriguing as well. On the day of his son Bruce&apos;s birth, he went to Severence Hospital to visit his wife and their newborn. At the time, the Declaration of Independence was being printed in great fervor. When the Japanese police raided the hospital because of this, the nurses hid the document under Bruce&apos;s cradle, and this was later discovered by Taylor. This event occurred just the day before the March First Independence Movement. He brought the document out from the hospital by stealth, and published a news report. \r\n\r\nWhile living in Dilkusha, he invited foreign missionaries and businessmen living in Gyeongseong to discuss the state of affairs in Korea. \r\n\r\nAfter his deportation, he died in the U.S. in 1948. According to his final wish, he was buried in Yanghwajin in Korea. \r\n\r\nHis son, Bruce Taylor, came to Korea in 2006 to visit his father&apos;s grave in Yanghwajin and for a tour of Dilkusha. Prior to his visit, there were various speculations surrounding this house; some claimed that it had been the office of Daehan Maeil Sinbo Newspaper, while others asserted that it had been the home of the missionaries. But Bruce Taylor&apos;s visit in 2006 ended these speculations. \r\n\r\nAcross from the house is the ginkgo tree planted by General Kwon Yul, and cultural heritage experts believe that the site originally contained the house of the general. ": 1,
    "Donuimun, the west great gate of the city wall, is commonly known as Seodaemun. Its construction was completed in 1422 (4th year of King Sejong&apos;s reign) and was torn down by the Japanese in 1915. The remnants were sold off as firewood for only 205 Korean won, which could just buy 17 bags of rice at the time.   \r\n\r\nThe west gate of the city wall was erected near Sajikdan Altar in 1396 (5th year of King Taejo&apos;s reign), but it was closed in 1413. Seojeongmun Gate was newly built to serve its role as the entrance of the city during King Taejong&apos;s reign. During the repair of the city wall in 1422, the gate was relocated to the south and it was named Donuimun. Because it was newly constructed at the time, it was also referred to as Saemun or Sinmun, which both means a new gate. Due the noise created by the people and horses traveling through the gate, King Yeongjo, who stayed in Gyeonghuigung Palace, ordered that the gate be closed and that it be opened only on special occasions. \r\n\r\nIn the 1920s, the Donguimun Gate area was also known for the streetcars. The streetcars, which had been in operation since 1899, traveled between Donuimun Gate and Cheongnyangni. There were countless people around the gate area, where the final stop was located, and one could spot people falling off the streetcars when too many people attempted to climb aboard. \r\n\r\nWhere there are people, there are always good food. In the late 1920s, there was a shop selling saekdongbingsu, or colorful ice treats, near Donguimun Gate. This is what gave rise to the present-date patbingsu, or shaved ice with red beans, and gwailbingsu, or shaved ice with fruits. The ice treat was colorful as it contained ice shavings with yellow, blue and red water, and it is believed to have been sugar water containing food dyes. The bingsu shop had a bush clover fence and a wooden curtain to create shade, where people could cool down. Compared to the Japanese, Korean merchants had relatively little money, so they opted to sell the ice treats from thatched roof houses (huts). Even to this day, bingsu is a popular summer treat for people of all ages, trying to blow away the heat. ": 1,
    "Emperor Gojong held the cup to his lips and let the coffee flow into his mouth. The bitter drink seemed to ease away the headache. &quot;Must I drink this bitter coffee to ease the agony of reality?&quot; \r\nThe Emperor lamented on the then-state of affairs, with the Japanese began meddling in everything after the Russo-Japanese War ended in Russia&apos;s defeat, \r\n\r\nWhen left with no choice, Emperor Gojong and Empress Myeongseong sought assistance from Russia to prevent the Japanese from encroaching on their territory even further. The Japanese responded with a brutal assassination of Empress Myeongseong, which is known as the Eulmi Incident. This resulted in Agwan Paacheon, or the Korea royal refuge at the Russian legation, with Emperor Gojong abandoning Gyeongbokgung Palace. \r\n\r\nHowever, tension escalated even further. Russia demanded the right to a gold mine in exchange for protecting the Emperor, while the U.S. and France demanded the right to install the railway and the electrical system. During this time, Gojong found a sense of comfort and solace in coffee, which he tried for the first time while taking refuge in the Russian legation. He soon became a coffee enthusiast and the only enjoyment he ever felt was when he was drinking coffee with the crown prince. \r\n\r\nThe old Russian legation was built in Renaissance architectural style. Seated on the highest hill of Jeong-dong, it has the view of Gyeongbokgung and Gyeongungung palaces as well as the legations of other countries. Unfortunately, only the watchtower remains today. The white building stands in solitude as if to convey the emotions felt by Emperor Gojong. ": 1,
    "Ha Ran-sa was the first Korean woman to acquire a degree in the U.S. Her maiden name was Kim, but she took her husband&apos;s last name after getting married. With great scholastic ambitions, she wanted to further her education and so, she submitted an enrollment application to Ewha Girls&apos; High School for enrollment. But her application was rejected three times because she was a married woman. She was relentless though, and sought other means to enter the school of her choice. She lit a lamp and went to Ewha Girls&apos; High School to meet the schoolmaster, L.E. Frey. When the schoolmaster asked her why she came, she turned off the lamp. \r\n\r\n&quot;Why did you turn off the lamp? It&apos;s dark here.&quot;\r\n&quot;My life is just as dark as this. Please lift the darkness from my life with the light of education.&quot; \r\nHer statement left a strong impression on Frey, who accepted her into the school. \r\n\r\nHer husband, Ha Sang-gi, provided his utmost support for her. When it was past the time she was supposed to return home from school, he ordered a servant to serve her dinner at school. Thanks to his support, Ha Ran-sa was even able to study in the U.S. In 1910, when an undergraduate program was launched at Ewha, she became the sole professor who was a Korean national. While working as the housemaster of the dormitory, she became known as a &quot;tigress&quot; to be feared with, and there wasn&apos;t a student who wasn&apos;t scolded by the stern housemaster and professor. \r\n\r\nAt the museum, you can take a glimpse into what it was like to graduate from Ewha. At the time, there was no such thing as a graduation ceremony; the school administrators, instead, introduced the graduates to affluent families for marriage purposes and they issued marriage certificates, which showed that the women were graduates of Ewha Girls&apos; High School. ": 1,
    "In the Joseon Dynasty, there were countless markets inside the city wall, with one in every neighborhood. Chipae Market, which was one of the three major markets in Seoul at the time, was seated along the present-day Tongil-ro which can be reached from the office of the Korea Chamber of Commerce & Industry near Sungnyemun by crossing the Yeomcheongyo Bridge. There, you will find a landmark stone marking the site of Chilpae Market. Chilpae was named so based on the division of the Hanseong region into 8 &quot;pae&quot; for patrol by Hunnyeondogam, Geumwiyeong and Eoyeongcheong in the late Joseon Dynasty. &quot;Pae&quot; refers to a small military unit, comprised of 40 to 50 officers, and it is between the size of a present-day platoon and company.  \r\n\r\nIt was easy to bring seafood into Chilpae as it was situated in the vicinity of Yongsan and Mapo. This is evident in &quot;Hanyang Song,&quot; describing the history and appearance of Hanyang in the mid 19th century. It goes &quot;There are a variety of fish in Chilpae,&quot; before describing various seafoods that can be obtained here including freshwater fish, bream, slender shad, octopus, conch, shellfish, shrimp, gizzard shad and more. \r\n\r\nChilpae was notorious for cornering and hoarding. In an old book, it reads, &quot;Unauthorized selling booths are installed all over Chilpae, outside the south gate, by merchants who arrive the morning and leave by nightfall. It is a jungle packed with people and horses, and everyone shamelessly engage in rampant selling.&quot; The merchants in this area purchased seafood directly from the rural areas or when the products were en route to Hanyang and engaged in wholesale trade. They raised and lowered the prices as they pleased, and this had a negative impact on the authorized sellers. \r\n\r\nThere were many imitation goods as well. The records show that merchants &quot;sold nickel as silver, goat horns as tortoise shell, and dog leather as sable leather.&quot; ": 1,
    "In 1395, Taejo Yi Seong-gye ordered the construction of the city wall, which lasted until September 1398 (7th year of King Taejo&apos;s reign). When naming the four great gates, King Taejo reflected the principles of benevolence, righteousness, propriety and wisdom, which was befitting as Confucianism was deeply rooted into Korean culture. \r\n\r\nBenevolence, righteousness, propriety and wisdom are pronounced in Sino-Korean as In, Ui, Ye and Ji, respectively, and they were incorporated into the names of the great gates. Dongdaemun was named Heunginmun, Seodaemun Donuimun, Namdaemun Sungnyemun, and finally Bukdaemun was named Sukcheongmun, where the concept of Cheong, which means clear, was similar to that of Ji, meaning wisdom. Sukcheongmun was later renamed as Sukjeongmun, but the exact date of the renaming is not known. The name, Sukjeongmun, first began appearing in the records in 1523 (18th year of King Jungjong&apos;s reign). Sukjeongmun was also referred to as Bukjeongmun at the time, and it is believed that the combined use of Sukcheongmun and Bukjeongmun to refer to this particular gate resulted in the name, Sukjeongmun. \r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nOn the other hand, Sungnyemun Gate was given its name because of pungsujiri or geomancy. Gyeongbokgung Palace, the main palace of Joseon Dynasty, faces Gwanaksan Mountain, and according to the pungsujiri principles, the fire energy of Gwanaksan Mountain was very intense. For this reason, Gyeongbokgung Palace was built to turn slightly away from the fiery mountain. \r\n \r\nStatues of Haetae, a mythical creature, were placed on the sides of Gwanghwamun Gate, the main gate of Gyeongbokguk Palace, as a means to suppress the fire energy energy emanated by Gwanaksan Mountain. Traditionally, Haetae was believed to bring and exude water energy. \r\n\r\nThere was a need to create fire energy that was stronger than that of Gwanaksan Mountain, and this was the reason the south great gate was given the name, Sungnyemun. The Chinese character for Sung is reminiscent of fire, while the Chinese character for Ye represents fire in the principles of yin-yang and the five elements. When placing these two characters on top of each other, it appears as though there is an intense fire burning. So, Sungnyemun is a name given to prevent the fire energy of Gwanaksan Mountain from reaching inside the city wall by overpowering it with a greater fire. Sungnyemun Gate, which was built to fight fire, was unfortunately burnt down in the recent years, which is very ironic. ": 1,
    "While taking a stroll in Namsan Mountain, you will soon come across the An Jung-geun Memorial Hall, which has remained relatively obscure. Most people, upon sighting the memorial hall, enter it without any great expectations, but they all leave with a strong impression.   \r\n\r\nThe An Jung-geun Memorial Hall was built to honor the independent activist, who assassinated Hirobumi Ito at the Harbin train station in Manchuria in October 1909. The original memorial hall, built by An Jung-geun Memorial Association in 1970, was demolished and a new memorial hall was erected on October 26, 2010. Upon entering, visitors will be welcomed by the seated statue of An Jung-geun in the central hall along with Taegeukgi, the national flag of Korea. This particular flag holds a symbolic value, as An cut his little finger and used the blood drawn to write  &quot;Independent Korea&quot; on it. The An Jung-geun Memorial Hall is a must-visit place, even for those who have little or no interest in history, and parents are urged to visit this place with their children as it will instill good values.\r\n\r\nWhile touring the memorial hall, you will discover information that you were previously unaware of. Until now, it was popularly believed that patriot An wrote the words in blood directly using his fingers, but actually, he used a brush. Also, the account of the assassination of Hirobumi Ito, considered as a public enemy by An, can be viewed inside Exhibition Hall 3. \r\n\r\nOn the third floor, inside the glass showcase on the left side, there is a model reenactment of the assassination at Harbin train station. When movement is detected as a visitor enters the area, the figures begin to move and the audio starts to play in the background. Walk a bit further, and you will see a model depicting An Jung-geun on trial for the assassination. ": 1,
    "On a clear fall day, a thin and white strip of smoke exited out of the Namsan (Mongmyeoksan) Beacon Mound and rose up to the clouds before disappearing into the sky. The farmer cutting down rice plants on the foothills of Namsan Mountain and the nobleman reading a book inside the Cheonghakdong Pavilion have smiles on their faces as they watch this sight. \r\n\r\nA beacon is a means of communication that is installed to inform the central agency of emergencies and urgent situations occurring in the border areas and vice versa to make swift responses to avoid crisis situations. There was a set of five beacons, producing smoke as a signal for various situations: smoke was produced once on a regular basis, twice upon sighting enemy forces, thrice when the enemies are approaching the boundaries, four times upon infiltration and five times upon the commencement of a battle. Mongmyeoksan Beacon Mound is considered as the last of the beacons, which are located nationwide. One stream of smoke from these beacons represent a time of peace and calm, when the people can focus on their day-to-day activities without worrying about survival. \r\n\r\nKing Taejo recognized the value of Namsan Mountain as a strategic point for the mliitary, and in 1396, he built a city wall in this area to provide a defensive barrier against foreign invasions. The beacon mound, in particular, was constructed in 1406 (6th year of King Taejo&apos;s reign). Namsan Mountain was closed off to public access in the Joseon Dynasty because it provided an open view of the city wall.\r\n\r\nAfter a visit during the late period of the Joseon Dynasty, English explorer Isabella Bird Bishop wrote in the book, &quot;Korea and her Neighbors,&quot; that &quot;Seoul, surrounded by mountains, is most well-viewed from the beautiful Namsan Mountain.&quot; \r\n\r\nDue to its geographical location, Namsan Mountain was regarded as a holy and dignified mountain, for which rituals were performed, and from the royal family of Joseon, it was given the noble title of Jinsan, or a guardian mountain protecting the national territory and the royal capital. In December 1395 (4th year of King Taejo&apos;s reign), Baegak and Namsan mountains were invest with the titles of nobility as Jingukbaek of the Baegak mountain god and Great King Mongmyeok, respectively. \r\n\r\nAt the peak of Namsan Mountain, there is Mongmyeoksinsa Shrine, where rituals were held in spring and autumn until the mid Joseon Dynasty. According to Sejongsillokjiriji (Annals of King Sejong), &quot;Mongmyeoksinsa Shrine [was] situated at the summit of Namsan Mountain, where small rituals [were] performed.&quot; Because the shrine was the place where rituals organized by the state were performed, it was called Guksadang Shrine. The shrine was torn down for the establishment of the Japanese shrine and it was newly built in Inwangsan Mountain. ": 1,
    "The Namsan Palgakjeong Pavilion overlooks the city center of Seoul. It boasts a stunning view, but for many, it reminds them of the tragic history of Namsan as the foothills of the mountain bear witness to the events where the nation and its people were ravaged. \r\n\r\nPalgakjeong Pavilion seems to be an ordinary pavilion where one can take in a view of the city, but its history is anything but ordinary. This was the location of  Guksadang Shrine, which was built to worship the mountain god of Namsan Mountain when King Taejo of Joseon chose Hanyang as the capital city. Guksadang Shrine at the time was the most important shrine in the country. \r\n\r\nHowever, Guksadang Shrine had to be relocated to Inwangsan Mountain during the Japanese occupation period to create space for a Japanese shrine. In 1925, the Japanese removed Guksadang Shrine from its original location, where it remained for over 500 years, claiming that it wasn&apos;t proper for a shrine of a colony to be in the same site as their shrine, where they worshipped their Emperor. \r\n\r\nNamsan Mountain has transformed into a public park since then, but nearly a century ago, the mountain symbolizing the greatness of Joseon and the royal family suffered at the hands of the Japanese. On the foothills of the mountain, there lies Dongpyeonggwan, where the Japanese envoys stayed during the period between the early Joseon Dynasty and before Imjinwaeran or the Japanese Invasion of Korea in 1592. During Imjiwaeran, the Japanese set up camp here. It was called Waejangteo, or the site of Japanese generals, as it served as the residence of Japanese generals including Nagamori Mashita, and the camps extended all the way to the present-day Jeongdong. \r\n\r\nIn 1884, the Japanese consular office was built on the foothills of Namsan Mountain, which contained the legation, Residency-General and Government General offices and others. The Japanese settled in the Jingogae area encompassing Yejang-dong, Juja-dong and Chungmuro 1-ga before expanding into the area around Sungnyemun, Hoehyeon-dong, Myeong-dong and Euljiro. People of Joseon, living in Bukchon, admired the exuberant commercial district of the Japanese in Namchon. \r\n\r\nThe present-day Seoul Youth Hostel was the site of legation office and the Residency-General&apos;s residence, while the Seoul Animation Center was the site of the Residency-General Office and Government General&apos;s residence. Namsan Mountain could be described as the heart of the Japanese settlement before the Government General office and residence were relocated to Gyeongbokgung Palace. ": 1,
    "In the late 1880s, cholera became prevalent inside the city wall. It infected countless people, causing hundreds of deaths on a daily basis. The future looked grim for many of the residents. One day, William Benton Scranton, a missionary doctor who founded Ewha Girls&apos; High School, saw a large number of kids abandoned outside Gwanghuimun Gate. Surprised by this sight, he asked one of the guides, &quot;Why are these kids outside the gate?&quot; The guide answered, &quot;They are infected with cholera. They were placed outside the gate to prevent transmission.&quot; Scranton, surprised by the guide&apos;s response, replied, &quot;If they are indeed infected with this deadly disease, shouldn&apos;t they be treated at home, and not be driven outside the city?&quot; \r\n\r\nAppalled by what was happening, Scranton brought one of the kids home to treat her, and began sending her to Ewha Girls&apos; High School. \r\n\r\nIn Joseon Dynasty, grave sites could not be formed inside the city wall. The deceased had to be carried outside the city through Souimun and Gwanghuimun gates. This is the reason Gwanghuimun was also called Sigumun to signify that it was a gate through which corpses passed through. \r\n\r\nThere are many heart-wrenching stories related to Gwanghuimun. \r\nThee was a time when the stone dust from Sigumun was considered as a cure for all diseases. For this reason, people living in the rural areas told those traveling to Hanyang, &quot;Scrape off and bring back some stone dust from Sigumun Gate in Hanyang.&quot; This myth arose from the belief that there wasn&apos;t a disease in the world that could surpass the suffering that Sigumun was under. \r\n\r\nThere was an important incident that occurred in 1907. The Japanese tried to force the dissolution of the Joseon army, but it was met with defiance and a street battle ensued. Sadly, the battle ended in defeat for the Joseon army, and the Japanese placed 120 dead bodies of the officers outside Gwanghuimun Gate and told the bereaved families to retrieve the dead bodies on their own. It&apos;s been said that the sounds of people crying were heard for days. Such heart-breaking stories aren&apos;t befitting of its name, Gwanghuimun, which means the gate of hope and bright future. ": 1
